  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1040 PM MDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nAnother night, another round of severe thunderstorms and heavy\nrain. The good news is that the upper level trough and surface\nconvergence that has lead to multiple nights of severe weather are\nfinally moving to the east, but not before we could see one more\nround of strong thunderstorms on Monday. The main threat for \nthese strong thunderstorms will be in Hudspeth County. Most of the\nregion will stay rain free on Monday. High temperatures on Monday\nwill run a few degrees below average.\n\nFor Tuesday and Wednesday we will see drier air move into the area\non a zonal (west to east) flow aloft. We look to stay dry both\ndays with high temperatures climbing a few degrees above average \nby Wednesday. Wednesday afternoon our upper level flow will begin \nto turn more to the southeast as our next upper level trough \napproaches the west coast. Our precipitable water values (PW's) \nwill drop below 0.5 for most of the area on Tuesday and Wednesday.\nBut then on Thursday, we will see another surge of moisture from \nthe south and southeast and we will see PW's jump to around 0.80 \nto 0.90 by Thursday. With the moisture in place and the next upper\nlevel trough approaching the area will be enough to give us a \nchance for thunderstorms Thursday afternoon and evening. On Friday\nthe main energy from the upper level trough will sweep across New\nMexico and that will give us a good chance for thunderstorms. \nWith the well above average PW's we could see some heavy rain \nmakers which can lead to isolated flash flooding. High \ntemperatures for both Thursday and Friday will run near seasonal \naverages. Right now next Saturday and Sunday look dry, with high \ntemperatures near average.",
